Fix the RTL8153B on USB 2.0 and tune r8152 for low latency (LG lm21u TV).

LG's r8152 (v1.08.2) detects the RTL8153B (versions 8/9) but programs it
like the RTL8153A. On the TV (OLED A1, MStar EHCI, USB 2.0) that caused:

- Lost bursts: USB_RX_EARLY_SIZE counts 8-byte units on the RTL8153B and
  the driver wrote 4-byte units, so the chip built aggregates up to twice
  the URB size. A frame crossing the URB end was dropped silently and the
  next URB was parsed from mid-frame: ~45% of game-stream-like UDP bursts
  (80-200 Mbit/s, each video frame sent at gigabit line rate) were lost
  after they had crossed USB. Now /8, as upstream (65b82d696b9e).
- Latency: the RTL8153B's RX timer is USB_RX_EXTRA_AGGR_TMR; the driver
  put 250 us into USB_RX_EARLY_TIMEOUT instead. Now EARLY_TIMEOUT = 128 ns,
  EXTRA_AGGR_TMR = 15 us and USB_UPT_RXDMA_OWN latches the change, as
  upstream. Other RTL8153B init from upstream: U2P3 off (809a7fc6593f),
  no RTL8153A MAC clock speed-down table (4b5dc1a94d4f), LPM U1/U2 only at
  SuperSpeed (a0246dafe684), RX_THR_B, BMU reset, test IO off on 32QFN.
- RX buffers: 32 x 16 KiB (was 10 x 16 KiB). 16 KiB fits one EHCI qTD.

Measured on the TV (PC on the same gigabit switch), before -> after:
TCP 220 -> 307 Mbit/s; UDP bursts 80-200 Mbit/s: 41-44% -> 0% lost
(300 Mbit/s @120 fps: 0.3%); UDP RTT p50 idle 0.35 -> 0.23 ms, under a
100 Mbit/s stream 0.45 -> 0.23 ms; CPU for 150 Mbit/s ~10% of one core.

Also: upstream fixes (NAPI disable ordering, RX budget, EPROTO handling,
checksum flags, TX watchdog stamp, napi_complete_done), RX buffers kept
across ifdown/ifup with a smaller-buffer fallback, a link report and RX
diagnostics in the ethtool statistics. RTL8152/RTL8153A programming is
unchanged. Module parameters (all optional) exist for A/B tests: see
docs/DEVELOPMENT.md.

